<interrogationes unitas="tempus">

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does tempus_nunc return, and can I use it as a date or timestamp?
    <responsum symbolum="tempus_nunc tempus_initiare fenestra_tempus_obtinere_pulsus mensura_momentum_nunc fasti_nunc">Seconds
      (f64) since the global clock started, which happens lazily on the
      first tempus_* call (or explicitly with tempus_initiare). It
      counts the platform's high-resolution pulses
      (fenestra_tempus_obtinere_pulsus), so it is for measuring
      intervals, timeouts and animation, not wall-clock time. For
      milliseconds since the Unix epoch use mensura_momentum_nunc; for
      calendar dates use fasti.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I run a frame loop at a fixed frame rate and read the frame delta?
    <responsum symbolum="tempus_ponere_quadra_destinata tempus_quadrum tempus_delta tempus_exspectare_quadrum tempus_obtinere_statisticas">Set
      the rate once with tempus_ponere_quadra_destinata (default 60).
      Each frame call tempus_quadrum first: it records the frame start
      and updates the delta that tempus_delta returns. After the work,
      tempus_exspectare_quadrum sleeps until 1/rate seconds after that
      tempus_quadrum (coarse sleeps, then 100-microsecond naps for the
      last two milliseconds); a rate of 0 or less disables the wait.
      tempus_obtinere_statisticas gives the current frame rate, the
      mean rate over the last CXX frames, and the minimum and maximum
      frame times in seconds. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): the
      mean rate comes back inf when two tempus_quadrum calls land in
      the same clock tick (delta 0, common in a tight loop), and a
      target below about 2.3e-4 fps (a frame period over about 4295 s)
      overflows the sleep conversion in tempus_exspectare_quadrum,
      which is undefined behaviour.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I call tempus_initiare again to restart the clock?
    <responsum symbolum="tempus_initiare tempus_creare tempus_restituere tempus_elapsum tempus_actualizare">You
      can, but it resets everything global: the start time, every frame
      statistic and the target rate (back to 60), so a rate set earlier
      is lost. To time something on its own, make an independent clock
      with tempus_creare(piscina), restart it with tempus_restituere,
      read it with tempus_elapsum, and call tempus_actualizare per step
      to fill its delta_tempus and tempus_totale.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I animate a value smoothly toward a target?
    <responsum symbolum="animare_ad elastrum_ad interpolare gradus_lenis lenire_cubicus_egressus remappare">animare_ad
      moves at a constant speed (celeritas times the delta) and lands
      exactly on the target. elastrum_ad is a damped spring: keep the
      velocity in your own f32 and pass its address each frame. For a
      timed tween compute t from 0 to 1, shape it with one of the
      lenire_* easing curves and feed it to interpolare; gradus_lenis is
      smoothstep and remappare maps one range onto another. Caution
      (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): elastrum_ad is explicit Euler and
      blows up on large deltas, e.g. k=100, d=10 at a 0.25 s frame
      reaches -4e33 within 40 steps and k=10, d=30 at 0.1 s diverges,
      so it is only stable while damnum times delta stays below 2 and
      delta times sqrt(k) stays small.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass values outside 0..1 to the easing functions, or negative time to the unda waves?
    <responsum symbolum="lenire_linearis unda_triangulus unda_dens_serrae unda_quadratus oscillare cohibere">Nothing
      is clamped. The lenire_* curves are only defined for t in 0..1
      (clamp with cohibere first). The waves take fmod of t times the
      frequency, which keeps the sign of a negative t, so
      unda_triangulus(-0.25, 1, a) gives -2a, outside its range,
      unda_dens_serrae also leaves its range, unda_quadratus stays high
      for all negative t, and oscillare goes negative. Use non-negative
      time.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use circumvolvere to wrap any value into a range?
    <responsum symbolum="circumvolvere interpolare_angulum">Only
      values a few ranges away: it adds or subtracts the width in a
      loop, so the time grows with the distance, and it never returns
      when the width is 0 or when the value is so large that adding the
      width does not change the f32 (1e9 into 0..1). For far values
      wrap with fmod yourself and add the width when the result is
      negative. interpolare_angulum is no substitute: it does not wrap
      its result, and because C fmod keeps the sign it only takes the
      shorter way round when b - a is at least -pi (0 toward -4 goes
      the long way). Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): circumvolvere
      also never returns for a reversed range (minimum greater than
      maximum, e.g. circumvolvere(0.5, 1, 0)) or for an infinite
      value.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what colour layout does interpolare_colorem expect?
    <responsum symbolum="interpolare_colorem">A
      packed i32 with red in bits 0-7, green in 8-15, blue in 16-23 and
      alpha in 24-31; each channel is interpolated linearly and
      truncated. t is not clamped, so keep it in 0..1. Caution (audit
      2026-10-18, unfixed): for any result alpha of 0x80 or more (every
      opaque colour, e.g. 0xFF000000 toward 0xFF0000FF) the alpha byte
      is shifted left 24 as a signed int, which is undefined behaviour
      that only happens to give the right value.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what kind of noise does unda_tumultus produce?
    <responsum symbolum="unda_tumultus sors_seminare">White
      noise in [-1, 1) that is a pure function of t: time is cut into
      buckets of about 81 microseconds (floor of t times 12345.6789),
      and each bucket seeds sors, so the same t always gives the same
      value and neighbouring buckets are independent. It is not smooth
      noise.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
